|> Exercise
    handle=_factory_roof_functions_1_

    Find the formula for a function whose graph
    looks like this, again using the floor function
    ‘$\lfloor \cdot \rfloor$’ as a building block:

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch3_ex_factory_roof_stretched.svg

    |> Solution

        We would like to argue the correctness of
        the following two-step process (divide the
        input by $2$, apply the function from Exercise
        >>_floor_introduction_):

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch3_ex_factory_roof_stretched_solution.svg

        Indeed, the two graphs featured above differ
        only by a horizontal dilation; dividing the
        input by $2$ “undoes” the dilation, at which
        point it suffices to apply the function pictured
        in the second graph; having declared our method
        correct, the answer is thus...
        $$
        {x/2 - \lfloor x/2 \rfloor}
        $$
        ...as obtained by substituting “$x/2$” (the halved
        input) for of “$x$” in “$\,x - \lfloor x \rfloor$”
        (the formula for the function from Exercise >>_floor_introduction_).

        |> SolutionNote
            One can check the answer by typing “x/2 - floor(x/2)”
            in DESMOS. Viz:

            |> Image
                src=images/png_ch3_desmos_composition_0_c.jpg
                width=1400px

        |> SolutionNote
            Alternately, enter “f(x) = x - floor(x)” and
            then “f(x/2)”:

            |> Image
                src=images/png_ch3_desmos_composition_1_c.jpg
                width=1400px

            Or:

            |> Image
                src=images/png_ch3_desmos_composition_2_b.jpg
                width=1400px

            The above features the
            
            __composition__
            
            of functions $f$ and $g$; in more detail, if we 
            switch the “input tube” and “output tube” sides of
            a function...

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch3_f_box_inverted.svg

            ...(compared to the drawing at the top of the
            chapter), then the composition of 
            $f$ and $g$, written

            $$
            \f \circ g
            $$

            and read

            __“$f$ of $\hlfbk{}g$”__

            !! (mathematicians have to invent a notation for
            !! everything—the little circle “$\circ$” is called
            !! the _composition operator_,
            !! by the way) 
            is the function that you get by
            gluing $g$'s box to the right of $\f$'s box, like so:

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch3_ex_f_box_g_box.svg

            In other words, $g$'s output is passed on to $-f$
            for further processing. (A certain movie called
            “The Human Centipede” comes to mind.)

            (To be perfectly clear,

            |> Image
                src=images/svg_ch3_ex_f_circle_g_equals.svg

            $f \circ g$ is a _function_, defined as the
            above assemblage of “$g$ first, $f$ second”.)

        |> SolutionNote
            A formal definition of “$f \circ g$” can be given
            as

            |> Boxed
                $$
                {(f \circ g)(x) = f(g(x))}
                $$

            or as

            |> Boxed
                $$
                f \circ g = (x \ra f(g(x)))
                $$

            where one can also clarify that

            |> Boxed
                $$
                {\dom f \circ g = \{x\, \in\, \dom g:\, g(x)\, \in\, \dom f\}}
                $$

            which is to say that the domain of $f \circ g$
            consists of all $x$ such that: *(i)* $g(x)$
            exists (a.k.a, “$x \in \dom g$”) and,
            *(ii)* $f(g(x))$ exists (a.k.a., “$g(x) \in \dom f$”).

        |> SolutionNote
            Amusingly—or not—both sides of the afore-mentioned

            _|“$(f \circ g)(x) = f(g(x))$”|_

            are read

            _|“$f$ of $\hlfbk{}g$ of $x\hspace{0.1em}$”|_

            since “$f \circ g$” is read “$f$ of $g\rt{0.1}$”,
            and “$f(\dots)$” is read “$f$ of ...”.
